    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d698">

                <pb facs="0955" n="841" xml:id="pg841"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.415 Traders/3</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Under Secretary of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Cotton</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the British Ambassador</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Lindsay</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-12-23">December 23, 1930</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Ambassador</hi>: Referring to your note
                    of the nineteenth I should be very glad to consider such a draft as you mention
                    in that note in regard to the Australian matter, with the idea that the draft
                    should be completed promptly. I assume that your Australians have a draft in
                    mind of what they want to accomplish, and that the first draft of the draft will
                    come from you.</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">J. P. Cotton</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
